<h1>Screw Conveyors: Shafted, Shaftless &amp; Inclined</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A screw conveyor uses a rotating helical screw blade (auger) inside a trough or tube to move bulk materials from an inlet to a discharge point. Screw conveyors are the workhorses of bulk material handling, simple, reliable, enclosed, and capable of horizontal, inclined, and vertical transport. They move grain, flour, cement, chemical powders, plastic pellets, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food ingredients</a> through packaging plants, processing facilities, and industrial operations worldwide.</p>
<h2 id="shaft-heading">Shafted vs. Shaftless</h2>
<h3>Shafted Screw Conveyors</h3>
<p>Shafted screw conveyors have helical flights welded to a center pipe shaft. The shaft rotates in end bearings at the drive and tail ends, with intermediate hanger bearings supporting the shaft between standard screw sections on longer conveyors. The shaft provides structural rigidity and allows long spans. Shafted conveyors handle most free-flowing bulk materials: grain, sugar, flour, cement, plastic pellets, and chemical powders.</p>
<p>Design practice for shafted screw conveyors follows <a href="https://cemastore.com/product/cema-standard-no-350-pdf-screw-conveyors-for-bulk-materials-stds-300-352/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ANSI/CEMA Standard No. 350, Screw Conveyors for Bulk Materials</a>, which covers selection, capacity, horsepower, and component design. Capacity scales steeply with diameter. In the <a href="https://www.kwsmfg.com/engineering-guides/screw-conveyor/screw-conveyor-capacity/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">KWS screw conveyor capacity table</a>, a 9-inch screw at 45% trough loading moves 8.2 cubic feet per hour per RPM, while a 24-inch screw at 45% moves 164 cubic feet per hour per RPM. The same table covers diameters from 4 to 36 inches and sets a maximum RPM for each size and loading. Required RPM is the target volumetric capacity divided by the capacity at 1 RPM, adjusted for any reduced or special flight pitch.</p>
<h3>Shaftless Screw Conveyors</h3>
<p>Shaftless screw conveyors use a thicker, self-supporting spiral flight without a center shaft. The spiral rests on a replaceable trough liner and is driven from one end. <a href="https://www.kwsmfg.com/resources/features-and-benefits/shaftless-screw-conveyor-liners/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">KWS describes the liner</a> as the support and bearing surface for the spiral, offered in UHMW polyethylene, polyurethane, AR400 steel wear bars, and ceramic. Without a center shaft and hanger bearings, shaftless conveyors handle materials that would wrap around shafts or clog between flights and bearings: fibrous materials (rags, paper pulp), sticky materials (wet sludge, clay), stringy materials (hair, grass), and materials with large irregular particles.</p>
<p>Shaftless conveyors handle municipal sludge, dewatered biosolids, food waste, screening debris, and industrial slurries that shafted designs cannot. They usually cost more than a shafted conveyor of the same capacity. The cost drivers are the thicker spiral, the liner system, and heavier trough construction.</p>
<h2 id="orientation-heading">Horizontal vs. Inclined</h2>
<h3>Horizontal Screw Conveyors</h3>
<p>Horizontal screw conveyors are the most common configuration. They move material at level or slight inclines from inlet to discharge. KWS notes that loss in conveying efficiency is minimal on inclines up to 10 degrees. Trough loading (the percentage of trough cross-section filled with material) appears in CEMA-based capacity tables at 15%, 30%, and 45%. The lower figures apply to abrasive materials and the highest to light, free-flowing, non-abrasive materials. Higher loading increases capacity but also increases power consumption and wear.</p>
<p>Horizontal screw conveyors serve as feeders (metering material from bins and hoppers), transfer conveyors (moving material between processes), and mixing conveyors (blending materials during transport with ribbon or paddle flights). They feed <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/drag-chain-conveyors/">drag chain conveyors</a>, and packaging machines in food, chemical, and mineral processing.</p>
<h3>Inclined Screw Conveyors</h3>
<p>Inclined screw conveyors elevate material at angles above 10 degrees. As inclination increases, effective capacity decreases because material falls back between screw flights. The <a href="https://www.kwsmfg.com/engineering-guides/screw-conveyor/types-of-screw-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">KWS engineering guide</a> gives typical efficiency losses of 10 to 40% on inclines up to 20 degrees, 10 to 70% up to 30 degrees, and 30 to 90% up to 45 degrees. The wide ranges reflect the material, trough type, and screw pitch, so a supplier should size an inclined screw for the specific material. To compensate, inclined screws use higher RPM, a larger diameter, reduced pitch flights, or tubular housings that contain the material between flights.</p>
<h3>Vertical Screw Conveyors (Screw Elevators)</h3>
<p>Vertical screw conveyors lift material straight up. The screw rotates inside a tubular housing, and centrifugal force plus the flight geometry push material upward. KWS rates its vertical screw conveyors to elevate bulk materials up to 30 feet without internal bearings. They handle materials like grain, cement, and powders. They have a smaller footprint than inclined conveyors or <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a> but handle lower capacities and generate more material degradation than bucket elevators for the same elevation change.</p>
<h2 id="housing-heading">Trough vs. Tubular Housing</h2>
<h3>U-Trough (Open Top)</h3>
<p>Standard screw conveyors use a U-shaped trough with a removable cover. The U-trough provides easy access for cleaning, inspection, and maintenance. Covers bolt on (standard) or clamp on (quick-release for frequent cleaning). U-trough construction suits horizontal and slight-incline applications. Trough material: carbon steel (standard), stainless steel (<a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">food and sanitary</a>), or abrasion-resistant plate (abrasive materials).</p>
<h3>Tubular Housing</h3>
<p>Tubular screw conveyors enclose the screw in a round pipe. The pipe fully contains the material, preventing dust emission and material spillage. Tubular housing is required for inclined and vertical screw conveyors to prevent material fallback. It is also used for horizontal conveyors handling hazardous, dusty, or contamination-sensitive materials. Tubular construction costs more than U-trough and is harder to clean and inspect.</p>
<h3>Jacketed Troughs and Tubes</h3>
<p>Jacketed screw conveyors have a double-wall trough or tube with a cavity for heating or cooling fluid. Hot water, steam, or thermal oil circulates in the jacket to heat material during transport (drying, preheating, melting). Chilled water cools material (crystallization, solidification). Jacketed screws combine transport and thermal processing in a single unit, eliminating separate heating/cooling equipment. Chemical and food processing plants use jacketed screw conveyors for temperature-controlled material transport.</p>
