<p>Capacity and length depend on screw diameter, RPM, and material properties. See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Screw Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Food ingredient handling:</strong> Stainless steel screw conveyors move flour, sugar, spices, and dry ingredients from storage to mixers, blenders, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging machines</a>. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary screw conveyors</a> use polished stainless steel, tool-free disassembly, and CIP-capable construction. Screw feeders meter precise quantities of ingredients into batch mixers and continuous blenders.</p>
<p><strong>Cement and concrete batching:</strong> Screw conveyors transfer cement from silos to weigh hoppers and batch mixers. The enclosed screw contains cement dust and provides controlled feed rates. Cement handling requires abrasion-resistant flights and trough liners due to the material's abrasive nature. Screw diameter is set by the required feed rate and the trough loading allowed for abrasive cement.</p>
<p><strong>Chemical processing:</strong> Screw conveyors handle chemical powders, catalysts, carbon black, and mineral fillers between reactors, dryers, and packaging. Jacketed screws provide heating or cooling during transport. Enclosed tubular designs contain hazardous dusts. Material compatibility (corrosion, chemical attack) dictates construction materials: 304 SS, 316 SS, Hastelloy, or polymer-lined carbon steel.</p>
<p><strong>Wastewater treatment:</strong> Shaftless screw conveyors move screening debris, grit, dewatered sludge, and biosolids in wastewater treatment plants. The shaftless design prevents wrapping by rags, hair, and fibrous debris that jam shafted conveyors. Stainless steel or coated carbon steel construction resists the corrosive wastewater environment.</p>
<p><strong>Grain and feed milling:</strong> Screw conveyors move grain, ground meal, and feed between storage, grinding, mixing, pelleting, and bagging. They feed <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a> for vertical transport and <a href="/conveyor-types/drag-chain-conveyors/">drag chain conveyors</a> for long horizontal runs. Screw feeders meter ingredient flow into mixers and extruders.</p>
<p><strong>Plastics processing:</strong> Screw conveyors feed plastic pellets, regrind, and additives from storage hoppers to extruders, injection molders, and compounding equipment. The metering accuracy of screw feeders provides consistent feed rates critical for process control. <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">Chain conveyor</a> and pneumatic alternatives handle longer distances but screw conveyors dominate short-run feeding applications.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Screw Conveyor</h2>
<p><strong>Material characteristics are the starting point.</strong> Free-flowing dry powders and granules: shafted screw in U-trough. Sticky, fibrous, or stringy materials: shaftless screw. Hazardous or dust-sensitive materials: tubular housing. CEMA 350 bulk material tables, and manufacturer guides built on them, list each material's bulk density, recommended trough loading, material factor for horsepower, component series, abrasiveness, and flowability. That material data drives screw component selection.</p>
<p><strong>Capacity determines screw diameter and RPM.</strong> Calculate required volumetric capacity (cubic feet per hour). Select screw diameter from CEMA capacity tables based on material classification and desired trough loading percentage. Calculate RPM to achieve the target capacity. Higher RPM increases capacity but also increases wear and power consumption. CEMA recommends maximum RPM limits based on screw diameter.</p>
<p><strong>Length and inclination affect power and component sizing.</strong> Longer conveyors need more horsepower, more hanger bearings, and larger shaft diameters to handle the cumulative material load. Inclined conveyors need more power per foot and may need modified flight pitch or tubular housing. Vertical screw conveyors need the most power per ton and special inlet hopper designs to feed material into the upward-moving screw.</p>
<p><strong>Environment and sanitary requirements set the materials.</strong> Standard industrial: carbon steel with paint. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food grade</a>: 304 or 316 stainless steel with polished welds and sanitary flange connections. Corrosive chemical: stainless steel, Hastelloy, or polymer-lined. High temperature: alloy steel with high-temp bearings and seals.</p>
<p><strong>Compared to alternatives:</strong> Screw conveyors are simpler and cheaper than <a href="/conveyor-types/drag-chain-conveyors/">drag chain conveyors</a> for short runs but degrade material more. Screw conveyors cost less than pneumatic systems but handle shorter distances. <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">Bucket elevators</a> beat vertical screw conveyors on capacity and gentleness for vertical lifts. Choose based on distance, capacity, material sensitivity, and budget.</p>
