<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a screw conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A rotating helical screw blade (auger) inside a trough or tube that moves bulk materials from inlet to discharge. Shafted for free-flowing materials, shaftless for sticky/fibrous materials. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/">conveyor types guide</a> for all bulk handling options.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Shafted vs shaftless, which do I need?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Shafted for free-flowing dry bulk materials (grain, powder, pellets). Shaftless for sticky, fibrous, stringy, or wrapping materials (sludge, rags, wet grain). Shaftless costs more but prevents clogging. Use shafted unless the material demands shaftless.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What materials can a screw conveyor handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Most dry and semi-dry bulk materials: grain, flour, sugar, cement, chemicals, plastic pellets, wood chips, animal feed. Shaftless models handle sludge, biosolids, and fibrous materials. Not suitable for very large particles, highly abrasive materials at volume, or products needing zero degradation.</p>

</div>
</div>
